CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ return: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ nested class member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ pack expansion: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ argument: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ same class: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [stmt.return]/2, [dcl.init.list]/3.4, [dcl.init.aggr]/16: `return
{1, 2, 3};' for `struct IA { int v[2]; int w; }' aggregate-initializes the
result with brace elision; [over.ics.list]/8 makes `h({1, 2, 3})' viable.
The front end paired the clauses with IA's members one by one ("invalid
implicit conversion from 'signed int' to 'signed int [2l]'", bin53) and
found no match for h.  Variables (`IA a = {1, 2, 3};') already worked.
libstdc++'s basic_format_args::_S_types_to_pack (`return
{_S_to_enum<_Args>()...};' into std::array) was dropped.  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
